al divide is the lack of physical access to technology. In many developing countries, there is a shortage of computers, internet connectivity, and other technological devices. This hampers individuals' ability to participate in the digital world and take advantage of the opportunities it offers. Furthermore, even in developed countries, certain regions or marginalized communities may lack access to technology due to limited infrastructure or financial constraints. Affordability is another significant barrier that contributes to the digital divide. The cost of technology devices, such as computers and smartphones, can be prohibitive for individuals or families with limited financial resources. Additionally, the cost of internet services and data plans can be unaffordable for many, particularly in low-income communities. The high cost of technology acts as a barrier, preventing individuals from accessing vital online resources, educational opportunities, and job prospects. Moreover, digital literacy plays a crucial role in bridging or widening the digital divide. Even if individuals have access to technology, many may lack the necessary skills and knowledge to utilize it effectively. Digital literacy encompasses the ability to use digital tools, navigate online platforms, critically evaluate information, and protect oneself online. Without these skills, individuals may struggle to find employment, engage in online learning, or access important services available on digital platforms. To bridge the digital divide, governments, non-profit organizations, and technology companies need to collaborate to ensure equal access to technology for all. Efforts should be made to provide infrastructure and connectivity to underserved areas. Additionally, initiatives targeting affordable technology devices and internet services can help make technology more accessible to low-income communities. Furthermore, there should be a focus on providing digital literacy training and education to individuals who lack these skills. In conclusion, the digital divide is a complex issue that affects individuals and communities worldwide. It encompasses various factors, such as physical access, affordability, and digital literacy. Bridging this divide requires concerted efforts from governments, organizations, and technology companies to ensure equal access to technology and provide necessary support and training to those who need it. Only through addressing these issues can we create a more inclusive and equitable digital society.", "encoded_message": "The cyber gap alludes toward the split amid people or societies who hold admission towards gadgets and those who do not. Throughout current cyber time, entry into tech holds evolved gradually essential, as it impacts several elements of life, such such as education, job, and discourse. However, this separation is not only bound to material admission into gadgets but also comprehends components such such as price, computerized competence, and framework. One of the major considerations contributing upon the electronic divide is the lack of concrete entrance into tech. Throughout several aspiring countries, there is a deficiency of PCs, web connection, and additional digital machines. This restricts people's capacity to partake in the online domain and utilize edge of the openings it provides. Besides, even in progressive lands, some sectors or disadvantaged groups may shortage approach to innovation due to reduced framework or budgetary restrictions. Cost-effectiveness is a further considerable blockade that provides to the electronic difference. The price of innovation machines, such as desktops and cellphones, can be excessive for persons or households with constrained fiscal reserves. Plus, the outlay of online services and figures schemes can be steep for several, significantly in poor groups. The exorbitant price of innovation functions as a obstacle, inhibiting users out of reaching critical web tools, aca